    #      added_subject) go to <phase>/api/listing.tsv and are printed, one
    #      line per walked file to <phase>/api/files.tsv. Its control: a
    #      fixture crate this log writes (a pub module, a private module with a
    #      re-export and a renamed one, a cfg(test) module, a pub(crate) item, a
    #      doc comment and a string holding pub fn, a method on a type the
    #      crate does not export), its rows equal to the expected rows.
    #  GD. The per-file agreement: each walked file's lines matching
    #      ^[[:space:]]*pub (grep, through count_matches) equal the listing's
    #      rows whose pub token starts its line; a row whose pub follows other
    #      text on its line (none expected) is printed as such. Every .rs file
    #      under the archive is walked or lies under a cfg(test) module, with a
    #      control; every added commit is an ancestor of the phase commit.
    #  GE. T2, the doc build: RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings, every feature, the
    #      crate alone, no dependencies; no warning or error line, with a
    #      control; the documented items counted from <target>/main's
    #      doc/emend/all.html as its item links (href's last path segment's
    #      kind printed beside the total), with a control on a fixture page.
    #  GF. T3, the hygiene step (specs/0029 "MSRV (decision 1)", A): two clones
    #      of <repo> at the phase commit in <scratch>/w, the git environment
    #      unset for every git and the clone's own scripts/check.sh run from
    #      the clone (the gate runs against its own repository). The first
    #      prints PASS hygiene alone, the three pins read beside it by other
    #      forms (the channel line, the workflow's install and --toolchain
    #      words, the workspace's rust-version line), each 1.98.1. The second
    #      has the workspace's rust-version moved to 1.98.0 and staged: exit 1,
    #      the line "FAIL hygiene: " + the step's message (read from the phase
    #      commit's check.sh) + ": 1.98.1 1.98.1 1.98.0", nothing on stderr.
    #  GG. The status patterns name this phase's log directory and planning's
    #      specs/0029, each with a control.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 7 P4 from M3 part 7 P3's test script (at the phase
    # commit 09d2e0f, printed in records/logs/m3p7-p3/test.log; its change list
    # GA to GG is above), generated by
    # <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-p4/gen/test.py, which asserts each
    # replacement's count, and grepped for P3's subjects, hashes, listing,
    # doc build and clones over the body. P4 changes no Rust source and no
    # script of the repository: its one code-side file is README.md, which the
    # run log checks. What differs, and why:
    #  HA. The phase is Milestone 3 part 7 P4: records/logs/m3p7-p4; the script
    #      on the disk in <scripts>, its stderr in <phase>/out. The base is P3's
    #      record commit ("M3 part 7 P3 record:"); the doc commit and the code
    #      commit, the phase commit, by their full subjects, beginning "M3 part
    #      7 P4: ", the roles read back to <phase commit>~2. This log runs no
    #      cargo; the target root's export stays from the frame and nothing
    #      builds into it. Every rm -rf is on a directory this run made,
    #      written with ${var:?}.
    #  HB. The spec is specs/0029, pinned by its COMMITTED blob (2ddd2b06...,
    #      the hash planning's message gave for the doc commit), at the phase
    #      commit; the control refuses specs/0028's blob at the phase commit.
    #  HC. T1 (P4 ruling 4): part 6's three close-out scripts, copied to
    #      <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/carried, each equal to the text
    #      printed in records/logs/m3p6-close/push.log, ci-run.log and scan.log
    #      as the phase commit holds them: the block after the line "## script
    #      that wrote this log" (push, ci-run) or "## script" (scan), each line's
    #      four-space indent removed, the block ending at the first line neither
    #      indented nor empty, and its last line, which must be blank, dropped.
    #      A fixture log is the extraction's control.
    #  HD. T2: carried/counting.sh, the library the close-out sources, equal to
    #      the phase commit's scripts/counting.sh; part 6's copy
    #      (carried/counting-m3p6close.sh) equal to the library part 6's
    #      push.log printed, and the two differing on one line, line 158, the
    #      guarded removal of specs/0029 item 3.
    #  HE. T3 (P4 ruling 4): gen.py printed with its sha256 and run with
    #      python3 -I into <scratch>/w/regen: exit 0, three "wrote" lines,
    #      nothing on stderr; each script it writes equal to the one in
    #      <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close, with a control.
    #  HF. T4 (P4 ruling 4): each generated script's diff against part 6's,
    #      printed in full with its counts of removed and added lines.
    #  HG. T5: bash -n over each generated script; the three shape greps of
    #      specs/0001 over each, the fixture of the frame's section as their
    #      control; no non-ASCII byte, no U+2014 bytes and no home path in each,
    #      each form with a control on a fixture.
    #  HH. T6 (P4 ruling 3): in the generated push script the functions
    #      excel_record_ok, excel_record_refused and excel_record_show are
    #      defined once each, the pre-mode section is there once and calls the
    #      check once and its control once; neither the ci-run nor the scan
    #      script names the check. The push and scan scripts' base pattern
    #      resolves to one commit of the phase commit's history, part 6's
    #      close-out doc commit by subject, and the push script's record
    #      pattern to one, part 6's close-out record; the scan's tip pattern,
    #      read as text, is the close-out doc commit's subject as the working
    #      specs/0029 states it (planning's rulings during P4, uncommitted at
    #      this run, printed by hash).
    #  HI. The status patterns name this phase's log directory and planning's
    #      two uncommitted files, specs/0029 and ROADMAP.md, each with a
    #      control.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    PHASE_DIR=$(dirname "$SELF_DIR")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$PHASE_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$PHASE_DIR/out/test.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    T=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "${T:?}"' EXIT INT TERM HUP
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$T/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$T/counting.sh"
    
    export RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4
    KEEP_WAS_SET=$([ -n "${EMEND_KEEP_DIR+x}" ] && echo yes || echo no)
    unset EMEND_KEEP_DIR
    # Changes LL and LM (rulings 8 and 10): the phase's one target root, a
    # subdirectory per source tree; <repo> builds into main.
    BUILD_ROOT=$PHASE_DIR/target
    # Change HC: the close-out generator's directory, read only.
    CLOSE=$HOME/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT/main" || exit 2
    export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$BUILD_ROOT/main
    fail=0
    check() { echo "$1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    passed_matches() { [ "$passed" -gt 0 ] && [ "$passed" -eq "$nok" ]; }
    
    head_is() { [ "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$1" ]; }
    not_head() { if head_is "$1"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    full_sha() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'; }
    if full_sha "$C"; then :; else echo "the phase commit must be a full 40-hex sha, not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    if head_is "$C"; then :; else echo "HEAD is not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    
    # The entry guard: every change must be this phase's log directory or
    # planning's uncommitted spec or ROADMAP.md (change AG; change HI).
    OURS_RE='^((\?\?| ?[MARCD]) records/logs/m3p7-p4/| M specs/0029-engineering-close[.]md$| M ROADMAP[.]md$)'
    entry_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    entry_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS_RE" git status --porcelain)
    entry_clean() {
      [ "$entry_all" != error ] && [ "$entry_ours" != error ] &&
        [ "$entry_all" = "$entry_ours" ]
    }
    if entry_clean; then :; else
      echo "the working tree holds changes outside this phase's logs and planning's uncommitted files" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    
    scrub() {
      sed -E -e "s#$MAIN([^-A-Za-z0-9_.]|\$)#<repo>\\1#g" -e "s#$T#<scratch>/w#g" -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<target>#g" \
        -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$PHASE_DIR#<phase>#g" -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    echo "about: M3 part 7 P4 phase commit $C; HEAD at run: $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    # Change AH: every environment input this script reads, on a labelled line.
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_NOTE: $([ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ] && echo "set; its text is the rerun line below" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input (environment) EMEND_KEEP_DIR, when this script started: $KEEP_WAS_SET (unset for the whole log)"
    echo "input (environment) HOME: $([ -n "${HOME:-}" ] && echo "set; the scrub writes it <home>" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset)"
    echo
    echo "## the three shape greps, over this script, before its log (specs/0001)"
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    SHAPE_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$SHAPE_DIR/fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SHAPE_DIR/fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      SHAPE_RESULT_SELF=$n_self
      SHAPE_RESULT_FIX=$n_fix
      check "$shape: this script holds none" "$(yn [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_SELF" = 0 ])"
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_FIX" = 1 ])"
    done
    rm -rf "${SHAPE_DIR:?}"
    
    echo
    echo "## the spec, pinned by the hash of its committed blob (change HB; specs/0018 close-out ruling 4)"
    SPEC=specs/0029-engineering-close.md
    # The hash planning's message gave for the doc commit's blob, typed (change HB).
    SPEC_GAVE=2ddd2b06105267673097508662375f7053e4b63016e1b340b6d4589385c8256a
    SPEC_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_HEAD=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum; sha256sum $SPEC (the working tree's copy, printed only)"
    echo "  the phase commit's blob: sha256 $SPEC_HEAD"
    echo "  the hash planning's message gave: $SPEC_GAVE"
    echo "  the working tree's copy, planning's uncommitted rulings if any (change HB), not the pin: sha256 $SPEC_SHA"
    check "the spec at the phase commit is the blob planning's message hashed" "$(yn [ "$SPEC_HEAD" = "$SPEC_GAVE" ])"
    spec_ctl() { if [ "$(git show "$C:specs/0028-images-and-comments-second-half.md" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$SPEC_GAVE" ]; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses another spec's blob (specs/0028 at the phase commit)" "$(yn spec_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the phase's commits by role, read from git"
    check_fn() { local label=$1; shift; check "$label" "$(yn "$@")"; }
    by_subject() { git log --format='%H' -n 1 -F --grep="$1" "$C"; }
    REC=$(by_subject 'M3 part 7 P3 record: logs, the size record, the sample records, the API review record, the independent check')
    DOC1=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P4: commit planning docs (the P3 verification and rulings, ROADMAP, the API review record's corrections)")
    CODE1=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P4: the README (decision 4 A)")
    echo "command: git log --format=%H -n 1 -F --grep=<the role's subject> <phase commit>, for each role"
    echo "  P3's record commit (the base): ${REC:-none}"
    echo "  the doc commit: ${DOC1:-none}"
    echo "  the code commit (README.md, the phase commit): ${CODE1:-none}"
    echo "command: git rev-parse <phase commit>~0 to <phase commit>~2"
    for k in 0 1 2; do git rev-parse "$C~$k"; done | sed 's/^/    /'
    roles_in_order() {
      local r
      for r in "$REC" "$DOC1" "$CODE1"; do
        [ -n "$r" ] || return 1
      done
      [ "$CODE1" = "$C" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~1")" = "$DOC1" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~2")" = "$REC" ]
    }
    check_fn "the code commit, the doc commit and P3's record commit, each the next one's parent, the code commit the phase commit (change HA)" roles_in_order
    roles_ctl1() { local DOC1=$CODE1; if roles_in_order; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check_fn "control: roles_in_order refuses the code commit named as the doc commit" roles_ctl1
    git log --format='%s' "$REC..$C" >"$T/subjects"
    PREFIX_RE='^M3 part 7 P4: '
    nsub=$(count_lines "$T/subjects")
    nprefix=$(count_matches -E "$PREFIX_RE" "$T/subjects")
    echo "command: git log --format=%s <P3's record commit>..<phase commit>"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$T/subjects"
    echo "  subjects in the range: $nsub; beginning \"M3 part 7 P4: \": $nprefix"
    prefix_all() { [ "$nsub" -ge 1 ] && [ "$nsub" = "$nprefix" ]; }
    check_fn "every commit in the range carries the phase's subject prefix" prefix_all
    printf 'M3 part 7 P4: x\nM3 part 7 P5: y\nM3 part 7: z\n' >"$T/c-subjects"
    check "control: the same count finds one prefixed subject of three" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E "$PREFIX_RE" "$T/c-subjects")" = 1 ])"
    no_such_subject() { [ -z "$(by_subject 'M3 part 7 P4: no such subject anywhere')" ]; }
    check_fn "control: a subject no commit has resolves to nothing" no_such_subject
    echo "phase: Milestone 3 part 7 P4 (specs/0029-engineering-close.md) test.log; run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    if [ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ]; then
      echo "rerun: $RERUN_NOTE"
      echo "  the superseded files are kept in <home>/${PHASE_DIR#"$HOME"/}/superseded (change FA)"
      echo "command: sha256sum and wc -c over each superseded attempt named above"
      for sf in ${RERUN_FILES:-}; do
        if [ -f "$sf" ]; then
          printf '    %s  %s bytes  %s\n' "$(sha256sum "$sf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
            "$(wc -c <"$sf")" "$(basename "$sf")"
        else
          printf '    NO HASH EXISTS for %s: the file is not there at run time\n' \
            "$(basename "$sf")"
        fi
      done
      if [ -z "${RERUN_FILES:-}" ]; then
        echo "    RERUN_FILES was not set, so no attempt above is hashed by this run"
      fi
    fi
    n_cargo=$(pgrep -x cargo | wc -l)
    n_rustc=$(pgrep -x rustc | wc -l)
    echo "SERIALIZED: no other log run was in flight; cargo processes at entry: $n_cargo; rustc processes at entry: $n_rustc; this log runs no cargo (change HA)"
    echo "root: <repo> stands for the repository's top level, the directory this script cds to (it lies inside <home>, so it is scrubbed first); every repository file this log reads is read at the phase commit by git show, except planning's working specs/0029 (change HH)"
    echo "root: <home> stands for the home directory of the account that ran this script; <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close holds the close-out generator, its carried copies of part 6's scripts and the three scripts it wrote (read only here)"
    echo "root: <phase> stands for <home>/${PHASE_DIR#"$HOME"/}, this phase's directory on the disk; <phase>/superseded keeps a superseded attempt's files, <phase>/out holds this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    echo "root: <scripts> stands for <phase>/$(basename "$SELF_DIR"), the directory holding this script"
    echo "root: <scratch>/w stands for this run's mktemp -d working directory, removed at exit: scripts/counting.sh is read at the phase commit into <scratch>/w/counting.sh and sourced from there; <scratch>/w/printed the scripts extracted from part 6's logs; <scratch>/w/regen the scripts gen.py writes here; <scratch>/w/fx the fixtures of the controls"
    echo "root: <target> stands for <phase>/$(basename "$BUILD_ROOT"), the phase's one target root (specs/0001): nothing in this log builds into it"
    apart() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 1 ;; esac; case "$2/" in "$1"/*) return 1 ;; esac; return 0; }
    roots_distinct() {
      apart "$T" "$PHASE_DIR" && apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$T" "$BUILD_ROOT" &&
        apart "$MAIN" "$PHASE_DIR" && apart "$MAIN" "$T" && apart "$CLOSE" "$PHASE_DIR" && apart "$CLOSE" "$MAIN"
    }
    check "the directory roots other than <home> and <phase>'s own children are distinct, none inside another" "$(yn roots_distinct)"
    roots_ctl() { if apart "$CARGO_TARGET_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: apart refuses <target>/main against <target>, which holds it" "$(yn roots_ctl)"
    check "no cargo or rustc process was running at entry" "$(yn [ "$((n_cargo + n_rustc))" = 0 ])"
    echo "RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=$RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL; CARGO_TERM_COLOR=$CARGO_TERM_COLOR; CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=$CARGO_BUILD_JOBS; $(rustc --version)"
    echo "EMEND_KEEP_DIR set when this script started: $KEEP_WAS_SET; unset for the whole log: $([ -n "${EMEND_KEEP_DIR+x}" ] && echo no || echo yes)"
    echo "EMEND_KEEP_DIR: no command of this log sets it (change HA: this log runs no crate test and no cargo)"
    echo "at entry: status lines $entry_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec $entry_ours; the lines:"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    check "the phase commit given to this script is a full sha" "$(yn full_sha "$C")"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is HEAD" "$(yn head_is "$C")"
    check "control: the same comparison against the null commit says no" "$(yn not_head "$NULL_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison against a short sha of the real commit says no" \
      "$(yn not_head "$(printf '%s' "$C" | cut -c1-7)")"
    echo
    echo "## script that wrote this log"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$SELF"
    echo
    echo "## the shared counting library this log sources"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$T/counting.sh"
    echo
    echo "## counting controls"
    counting_controls
    if [ "$counting_controls_ok" != yes ]; then fail=1; fi
    
    # ynnot: for a condition that must NOT hold (specs/0001: a leading bang
    # inside yn runs a command named by the bang).
    ynnot() { if "$@"; then echo no; else echo yes; fi; }
    check "control: ynnot reports no on a passing command" "$(yn [ "$(ynnot true)" = no ])"
    TAB=$(printf '\t')
    mkdir -p "$T/printed" "$T/regen" "$T/fx" || fail=1
    NAMES="push ci scan"
    # log_of <name>: the part 6 close-out log that printed that script.
    log_of() { case "$1" in push) echo push ;; ci) echo ci-run ;; scan) echo scan ;; esac; }
    # heading_of <name>: the heading the script follows in that log.
    heading_of() { case "$1" in scan) echo "## script" ;; *) echo "## script that wrote this log" ;; esac; }
    # extract_printed <log file> <heading> <out>: the block after the heading,
    # each line's four-space indent removed, ending at the first line neither
    # indented nor empty. Prints nothing.
    extract_printed() {
      awk -v h="$2" '$0 == h && !d { p = 1; d = 1; next } p { if ($0 ~ /^    / || $0 == "") { sub(/^    /, ""); print } else exit }' "$1" >"$3"
    }
    # last_blank <file>: whether the file's last line is empty.
    last_blank() { [ -s "$1" ] && [ -z "$(tail -n 1 "$1")" ]; }
    # drop_last <file>: the file without its last line, in place.
    drop_last() { sed -i '$d' "$1"; }
    
    echo
    echo "## T1: part 6's close-out scripts, carried unchanged (specs/0029 P4 ruling 4; change HC)"
    echo "command: printf a fixture log (a heading, two indented lines, a blank line, an unindented line) to <scratch>/w/fx/log; extract_printed over it"
    printf '%s\n' 'x' '## script' '    a' '      b' '' 'after' >"$T/fx/log"
    extract_printed "$T/fx/log" "## script" "$T/fx/ext"
    printf '%s\n' 'a' '  b' '' >"$T/fx/ext-want"
    check "control: the extraction keeps the two lines without their indent and the blank line, and stops at the unindented line" "$(yn cmp -s "$T/fx/ext" "$T/fx/ext-want")"
    check "control: last_blank finds the fixture's blank last line" "$(yn last_blank "$T/fx/ext")"
    check "control: last_blank refuses the fixture's first line alone" "$(ynnot last_blank "$T/fx/log")"
    for n in $NAMES; do
      l=$(log_of "$n")
      h=$(heading_of "$n")
      echo "command: git show <phase commit>:records/logs/m3p6-close/$l.log > <scratch>/w/printed/$l.log; extract_printed <scratch>/w/printed/$l.log \"$h\" <scratch>/w/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh; its last line, blank, dropped"
      git show "$C:records/logs/m3p6-close/$l.log" >"$T/printed/$l.log"
      extract_printed "$T/printed/$l.log" "$h" "$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh"
      check "the block printed under \"$h\" in $l.log ends in a blank line" "$(yn last_blank "$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh")"
      drop_last "$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh"
      echo "  extracted: $(count_lines "$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      echo "  <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh: $(count_lines "$CLOSE/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$CLOSE/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      check "carried/m3p6close-$n.sh is the script part 6's $l.log printed, byte for byte" "$(yn cmp -s "$T/printed/m3p6close-$n.sh" "$CLOSE/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh")"
    done
    cp "$T/printed/m3p6close-push.sh" "$T/fx/c-push"; printf 'x\n' >>"$T/fx/c-push"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the extract with one line appended" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$T/fx/c-push" "$CLOSE/carried/m3p6close-push.sh")"
    
    echo
    echo "## T2: the counting library the close-out sources (change HD)"
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:scripts/counting.sh | cmp - <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/carried/counting.sh"
    echo "  the phase commit's scripts/counting.sh: sha256 $(sha256sum <"$T/counting.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    echo "  carried/counting.sh: sha256 $(sha256sum <"$CLOSE/carried/counting.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    check "carried/counting.sh is the phase commit's scripts/counting.sh" "$(yn cmp -s "$T/counting.sh" "$CLOSE/carried/counting.sh")"
    LIB_H="## the shared counting library this log sources"
    echo "command: extract_printed <scratch>/w/printed/push.log \"$LIB_H\" <scratch>/w/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh; its last line, blank, dropped"
    extract_printed "$T/printed/push.log" "$LIB_H" "$T/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh"
    check "the block printed under \"$LIB_H\" in part 6's push.log ends in a blank line" "$(yn last_blank "$T/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh")"
    drop_last "$T/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh"
    echo "  extracted: sha256 $(sha256sum <"$T/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1); carried/counting-m3p6close.sh: sha256 $(sha256sum <"$CLOSE/carried/counting-m3p6close.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    check "carried/counting-m3p6close.sh is the library part 6's push.log printed, byte for byte" "$(yn cmp -s "$T/printed/counting-m3p6close.sh" "$CLOSE/carried/counting-m3p6close.sh")"
    echo "command: diff carried/counting-m3p6close.sh carried/counting.sh"
    diff "$CLOSE/carried/counting-m3p6close.sh" "$CLOSE/carried/counting.sh" >"$T/lib.diff"
    lib_rc=$?
    echo "  exit: $lib_rc; the diff, its removal command word printed as rm -r[f] so that the scan's removal form does not read this print as a command:"
    sed -e 's/rm -rf/rm -r[f]/' -e 's/^/    /' "$T/lib.diff"
    # lib_one_line <diff>: the diff is one changed line, line 158, whose old text
    # holds the bare "$d" target and whose new text the guarded "${d:?}" one.
    lib_one_line() {
      [ "$(count_lines "$1")" = 4 ] && [ "$(sed -n 1p "$1")" = 158c158 ] &&
        [ "$(sed -n 2p "$1" | count_matches -F '"$d"' /dev/stdin)" = 1 ] &&
        [ "$(sed -n 4p "$1" | count_matches -F '"${d:?}"' /dev/stdin)" = 1 ]
    }
    check "the two libraries differ on line 158 alone, the guarded removal (specs/0029 item 3)" "$(yn lib_one_line "$T/lib.diff")"
    printf '158c158\n< a\n---\n> b\n' >"$T/fx/lib.diff"
    check "control: the same check refuses a one-line diff at line 158 whose lines hold neither target" "$(ynnot lib_one_line "$T/fx/lib.diff")"
    
    echo
    echo "## T3: gen.py, printed and run into a scratch directory (P4 ruling 4; change HE)"
    echo "command: sha256sum <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/gen.py; its text:"
    echo "    $(sha256sum <"$CLOSE/gen.py" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    sed 's/^/      /' "$CLOSE/gen.py"
    echo "command: python3 -I <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/gen.py <scratch>/w/regen"
    python3 -I "$CLOSE/gen.py" "$T/regen" >"$T/gen.out" 2>"$T/gen.err"
    gen_rc=$?
    echo "  exit: $gen_rc; standard error $(wc -c <"$T/gen.err") bytes; standard output:"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$T/gen.out"
    printf 'wrote m3p7close-push.sh\nwrote m3p7close-ci.sh\nwrote m3p7close-scan.sh\n' >"$T/gen.out-want"
    gen_ran() { [ "$gen_rc" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$T/gen.err" ] && cmp -s "$T/gen.out" "$T/gen.out-want"; }
    check "gen.py exits 0, nothing on stderr, its three wrote lines" "$(yn gen_ran)"
    for n in $NAMES; do
      echo "  m3p7close-$n.sh: regenerated sha256 $(sha256sum <"$T/regen/m3p7close-$n.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1); on the disk $(sha256sum <"$CLOSE/m3p7close-$n.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      check "the regenerated m3p7close-$n.sh is the one on the disk, byte for byte" "$(yn cmp -s "$T/regen/m3p7close-$n.sh" "$CLOSE/m3p7close-$n.sh")"
    done
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the regenerated push script against the ci-run script" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$T/regen/m3p7close-push.sh" "$CLOSE/m3p7close-ci.sh")"
    
    echo
    echo "## T4: each generated script's diff against part 6's, in full (P4 ruling 4; change HF)"
    for n in $NAMES; do
      echo "command: diff <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/m3p7close-$n.sh"
      diff "$CLOSE/carried/m3p6close-$n.sh" "$CLOSE/m3p7close-$n.sh" >"$T/$n.diff"
      d_rc=$?
      n_rm=$(count_matches -E '^< ' "$T/$n.diff")
      n_add=$(count_matches -E '^> ' "$T/$n.diff")
      echo "  exit: $d_rc; diff lines $(count_lines "$T/$n.diff"); removed (<) $n_rm; added (>) $n_add; the diff:"
      sed 's/^/    /' "$T/$n.diff"
      check "diff exits 1 for m3p7close-$n.sh: the scripts differ and diff read both" "$(yn [ "$d_rc" = 1 ])"
    done
    printf '1c1\n< a\n---\n> b\n' >"$T/fx/c.diff"
    check "control: the counting form finds one added line in a fixture diff" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E '^> ' "$T/fx/c.diff")" = 1 ])"
    
    echo
    echo "## T5: bash -n, the shape greps, and the bytes of each generated script (change HG)"
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$T/fx/shapes"
    EMD=$(printf '\342\200\224')
    printf 'a\n\303\251\n' >"$T/fx/nonascii"
    printf 'a %s b\n' "$EMD" >"$T/fx/emdash"
    printf 'a %s/x\n' "$HOME" >"$T/fx/home"
    for n in $NAMES; do
      f=$CLOSE/m3p7close-$n.sh
      echo "### m3p7close-$n.sh"
      echo "command: bash -n <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-close/m3p7close-$n.sh"
      bash -n "$f" >"$T/bn.out" 2>&1
      bn_rc=$?
      echo "  exit: $bn_rc; output $(wc -c <"$T/bn.out") bytes"
      bn_ok() { [ "$bn_rc" = 0 ] && [ ! -s "$T/bn.out" ]; }
      check "bash -n reads m3p7close-$n.sh with no error" "$(yn bn_ok)"
      for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
        var=SHAPE_$shape
        n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$f")
        echo "  shape $shape: $n_self lines"
        check "$shape: m3p7close-$n.sh holds none" "$(yn [ "$n_self" = 0 ])"
      done
      n_na=$(count_matches -P '[^\x00-\x7F]' "$f")
      n_em=$(count_matches -F "$EMD" "$f")
      n_hm=$(count_matches -F "$HOME/" "$f")
      echo "  lines with a non-ASCII byte: $n_na; lines with the U+2014 bytes: $n_em; lines naming the home directory: $n_hm"
      check "m3p7close-$n.sh holds no non-ASCII byte" "$(yn [ "$n_na" = 0 ])"
      check "m3p7close-$n.sh holds no U+2014 bytes" "$(yn [ "$n_em" = 0 ])"
      check "m3p7close-$n.sh names no home path" "$(yn [ "$n_hm" = 0 ])"
    done
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$T/fx/shapes")" = 1 ])"
    done
    check "control: the non-ASCII form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -P '[^\x00-\x7F]' "$T/fx/nonascii")" = 1 ])"
    check "control: the U+2014 form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -F "$EMD" "$T/fx/emdash")" = 1 ])"
    check "control: the home form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -F "$HOME/" "$T/fx/home")" = 1 ])"
    printf 'if then\n' >"$T/fx/bad.sh"
    # The fixture's parse error goes to a file of its own, not to this script's
    # standard error.
    bn_bad() { bash -n "$T/fx/bad.sh" 2>"$T/fx/bad.err"; }
    check "control: bash -n refuses a fixture that does not parse" "$(ynnot bn_bad)"
    echo "  the fixture's parse error, bytes: $(wc -c <"$T/fx/bad.err")"
    
    echo
    echo "## T6: the Excel-record check and the patterns (P4 ruling 3; change HH)"
    PUSH=$CLOSE/m3p7close-push.sh
    SCAN=$CLOSE/m3p7close-scan.sh
    CI=$CLOSE/m3p7close-ci.sh
    n_ok=$(count_matches -E '^excel_record_ok\(\) \{$' "$PUSH")
    n_rf=$(count_matches -E '^excel_record_refused\(\) ' "$PUSH")
    n_sh=$(count_matches -E '^excel_record_show\(\) \{$' "$PUSH")
    n_sec=$(count_matches -F 'echo "## the Excel check record at the tip (change 20)"' "$PUSH")
    n_call=$(count_matches -F '"$(yn excel_record_ok "$T/xr")"' "$PUSH")
    n_ctl=$(count_matches -F 'yn excel_record_refused "$T/xr-c")"' "$PUSH")
    echo "command: count_matches over m3p7close-push.sh: the three definitions; the section's heading line; the check's call; its control's call"
    echo "  excel_record_ok defined: $n_ok; excel_record_refused: $n_rf; excel_record_show: $n_sh; the section: $n_sec; the check's call: $n_call; the control's call: $n_ctl"
    excel_once() { [ "$n_ok" = 1 ] && [ "$n_rf" = 1 ] && [ "$n_sh" = 1 ] && [ "$n_sec" = 1 ] && [ "$n_call" = 1 ] && [ "$n_ctl" = 1 ]; }
    check "the push script defines the three functions once each and its pre mode holds the section, the check and its control once each" "$(yn excel_once)"
    excel_ctl() { local n_call=2; excel_once; }
    check "control: the same check refuses a second call" "$(ynnot excel_ctl)"
    n_other=$(count_matches -F 'excel_record' "$CI" "$SCAN")
    echo "  lines naming excel_record in the ci-run and scan scripts: $n_other"
    check "neither the ci-run nor the scan script names the check" "$(yn [ "$n_other" = 0 ])"
    # pattern_of <file> <NAME>: the single-quoted value of NAME='...' in the file.
    pattern_of() { sed -n "s/^$2='\\(.*\\)'\$/\\1/p" "$1"; }
    BASE_P=$(pattern_of "$PUSH" BASE_PATTERN)
    BASE_S=$(pattern_of "$SCAN" BASE_PATTERN)
    PREV_P=$(pattern_of "$PUSH" PREV_RECORD_PATTERN)
    TIP_S=$(pattern_of "$SCAN" TIP_PATTERN)
    echo "  the push script's BASE_PATTERN: $BASE_P"
    echo "  the scan script's BASE_PATTERN: $BASE_S"
    echo "  the push script's PREV_RECORD_PATTERN: $PREV_P"
    echo "  the scan script's TIP_PATTERN: $TIP_S"
    same_base() { [ -n "$BASE_P" ] && [ "$BASE_P" = "$BASE_S" ]; }
    check "the push and scan scripts carry the same base pattern" "$(yn same_base)"
    echo "command: git log --format='%H %s' --grep=<the pattern> <phase commit>, for the base and the record patterns"
    git log --format='%H %s' --grep="$BASE_P" "$C" >"$T/base-hits"
    git log --format='%H %s' --grep="$PREV_P" "$C" >"$T/prev-hits"
    sed 's/^/    base: /' "$T/base-hits"
    sed 's/^/    record: /' "$T/prev-hits"
    n_bh=$(count_lines "$T/base-hits")
    n_ph=$(count_lines "$T/prev-hits")
    check "the base pattern resolves to one commit of the phase commit's history" "$(yn [ "$n_bh" = 1 ])"
    check "the record pattern resolves to one commit of the phase commit's history" "$(yn [ "$n_ph" = 1 ])"
    n_b6=$(count_matches -F ' M3 part 6 close-out: commit planning docs ' "$T/base-hits")
    n_p6=$(count_matches -F ' M3 part 6 close-out record' "$T/prev-hits")
    check "that base commit is part 6's close-out doc commit, by its subject" "$(yn [ "$n_b6" = 1 ])"
    check "that record commit is part 6's close-out record commit, by its subject" "$(yn [ "$n_p6" = 1 ])"
    git log --format='%H' --grep="$TIP_S" "$C" >"$T/tip-hits"
    echo "  commits of the phase commit's history the tip pattern matches: $(count_lines "$T/tip-hits") (the close-out doc commit is made after P5)"
    check "the tip pattern matches no commit yet" "$(yn [ "$(count_lines "$T/tip-hits")" = 0 ])"
    # as_text <pattern>: the pattern's anchors and bracketed parentheses read as text.
    as_text() { printf '%s' "$1" | sed -e 's/^\^//' -e 's/\$$//' -e 's/\[(\]/(/g' -e 's/\[)\]/)/g'; }
    TIP_TEXT=$(as_text "$TIP_S")
    echo "  the tip pattern read as text: $TIP_TEXT"
    echo "command: sha256sum specs/0029-engineering-close.md (planning's working copy, the rulings during P4 uncommitted at this run); tr '\\n' ' ' over it; grep -o -F the text above"
    echo "  the working copy: sha256 $SPEC_SHA"
    tr '\n' ' ' <"$SPEC" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -F -e "$TIP_TEXT" >"$T/tip-in-spec"
    n_ts=$(count_lines "$T/tip-in-spec")
    echo "  times the working specs/0029 states it, its lines joined: $n_ts"
    check "the scan's tip pattern is the close-out doc commit's subject the working specs/0029 states" "$(yn [ "$n_ts" -ge 1 ])"
    printf 'see "M3 part 7\nclose-out: x (y)" here\n' >"$T/fx/wrapped"
    check "control: the joined form finds a subject wrapped over two lines" "$(yn [ "$(tr '\n' ' ' <"$T/fx/wrapped" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -F -e 'M3 part 7 close-out: x (y)' | count_lines /dev/stdin)" = 1 ])"
    check "control: as_text reads a bracketed pattern as its text" "$(yn [ "$(as_text '^a [(]b[)]$')" = 'a (b)' ])"
    echo
    echo "## status after the runs"
    echo "command: git status --porcelain"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    st_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    st_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS_RE" git status --porcelain)
    echo "status lines: $st_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted files: $st_ours"
    status_accounted() {
      [ "$st_all" != error ] && [ "$st_ours" != error ] && [ "$st_all" = "$st_ours" ]
    }
    check "every change in the working tree is one of this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted files" "$(yn status_accounted)"
    printf '?? records/logs/m3p7-p4/test.log\n M scripts/check.sh\n?? records/logs/m3p7-p3/test.log\n M ROADMAP.md\n M specs/0028-images-and-comments-second-half.md\n M specs/0029-engineering-close.md\n' >"$T/c-status"
    check "control: the same pattern finds three of six status lines, this phase's log and planning's two files (change HI)" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E "$OURS_RE" "$T/c-status")" = 3 ])"
    echo
    echo "## this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    nerr=$(wc -c <"$ERR")
    cp "$ERR" "$T/c-err"; printf 'x' >>"$T/c-err"
    nerr_ctl=$(wc -c <"$T/c-err")
    echo "  command: wc -c <phase>/out/test.stderr: $nerr bytes; a copy with one byte appended: $nerr_ctl"
    check "this script wrote nothing to its standard error" "$(yn [ "$nerr" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count reads one byte more in the copy" "$(yn [ "$nerr_ctl" = "$((nerr + 1))" ])"
    echo "run ended at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "test log checks all as expected: $([ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    exec 1>&3
    wait "$SCRUB_PID" 2>/dev/null
    exit "$fail"

## the shared counting library this log sources
    # emend shared counting forms (specs/0007-gate-self-test.md, "scripts/counting.sh").
    #
    # Sourced by scripts/gate-self-test.sh and by the log scripts of later phases,
    # which use these instead of carrying their own copy.
    #
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. In T3 P4 four counting sites compared a bare `grep -c`
    # with 0 without reading the exit status, so a read error printed 0 and the
    # check passed. Two independent checks found them, and the class was declared
    # closed three further times while instances still stood: a helper whose first
    # repair read grep's status while `cat` was the failing command, and both
    # halves of one function in T2 P7. Planning's remedy is that every counting
    # form, including a throwaway one, is a named function that reads the exit
    # status and has a positive control, from the start.
    #
    # Every function prints either a number or the word "error". None of them ever
    # prints a matched line, a value, or a path. A caller that compares a result
    # with 0 therefore cannot mistake a failed read for an absence.
    #
    # counting_controls runs every form against a match, a miss and an unreadable
    # file, prints one line per control, and sets counting_controls_ok. Callers run
    # it before using any form.
    
    G=/usr/bin/grep
    
    # count_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: total matching lines across the
    # files, or "error" if any file could not be read. The files go to grep
    # directly: with `cat file | grep -c`, an unreadable file makes CAT fail while
    # grep counts 0 over the empty stream and exits 1, so the read error reads as
    # "no hits".
    count_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_from_file <-F|-E> <pattern file> <file>...: the same, with the patterns
    # read from a file (grep -f), so a pattern is never printed.
    count_from_file() {
      local mode=$1 patterns=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -f "$patterns" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_bytes <tr class> <file>...: total bytes of that class across the files,
    # or "error" if any file could not be read. Each file is read on its own rather
    # than through `cat "$@" | tr`, for the same reason as above.
    count_bytes() {
      local class=$1 total=0 f n
      shift
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd "$class" <"$f" | wc -c) || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_lines <file>...: total lines, or "error" if any file could not be read.
    count_lines() {
      local total=0 f n
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(wc -l <"$f") || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_matches_i <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: count_matches with -i, the form
    # the hygiene step uses for OFFICE_RE and ZONE_RE. It lived in a log script as a
    # hand-rolled copy with no control of its own (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap
    # 14); a counting form belongs here, with a control, even a throwaway one.
    count_matches_i() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c -i "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # run_lines <command...>: run the command, and print the number of lines it
    # wrote on stdout, or "error" if the command itself failed. The log scripts
    # asserted a clean working tree with
    #   git status --porcelain >file 2>/dev/null || : >file
    # which turns a FAILED git status into an empty file, so the check read a
    # failed command as a clean tree (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap 1; planning
    # reproduced it). Reading the command's exit status is the whole point: a
    # command that could not run is never the same as a command that found nothing.
    run_lines() {
      local out rc
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l | tr -d ' '
    }
    
    # run_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <command...>: the same, counting only the lines
    # that match, so a caller can compare "all lines" with "lines of the expected
    # shape" without either count hiding a failed command.
    run_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" || true
    }
    
    CTRL_CLASS='\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177'
    NONASCII_CLASS='\200-\377'
    
    # counting_controls <label prefix>: run every counting form above against a
    # fixture that must match and one that must not, and against an unreadable
    # file, printing one line per result. Sets counting_controls_ok to yes or no.
    # This is the positive control planning requires for every form, and it also
    # proves each form reports "error" rather than 0 when it cannot read.
    counting_controls() {
      local d ok=yes r
      d=$(mktemp -d) || { counting_controls_ok=no; return; }
      printf 'alpha\nbeta needle gamma\nneedle\n' >"$d/hit"
      printf 'nothing of interest\n' >"$d/miss"
      printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m\017 y\ncaf\303\251\n' >"$d/bytes"
      printf 'needle\n' >"$d/patterns"
      printf 'unreadable\n' >"$d/locked"
      chmod 000 "$d/locked"
    
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit");        echo "  control count_matches on a file with two matching lines: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/miss");       echo "  control count_matches on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_matches summed over two files: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -E 'ne+dle' "$d/hit");      echo "  control count_matches with a regular expression: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/locked");     echo "  control count_matches on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_matches on readable plus unreadable: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/hit"); echo "  control count_from_file on the same file: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_from_file on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_from_file on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/bytes");    echo "  control count_bytes, control bytes in a file with ESC, ESC and SI: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$NONASCII_CLASS" "$d/bytes"); echo "  control count_bytes, non-ASCII bytes in a file with one two-byte character: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/miss");     echo "  control count_bytes on a clean file: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_bytes on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/hit");                    echo "  control count_lines on a three-line file: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/locked");                 echo "  control count_lines on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      printf 'NEEDLE\nneedle\n' >"$d/mixedcase"
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/mixedcase"); echo "  control count_matches_i on mixed case: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/mixedcase");  echo "  control count_matches on the same file, case sensitive: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_matches_i on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines printf 'a\nb\nc\n');            echo "  control run_lines on a command printing three lines: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines true);                          echo "  control run_lines on a command printing nothing: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines false);                         echo "  control run_lines on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines "$d/no-such-command");          echo "  control run_lines on a command that cannot run: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle printf 'needle\nother\n'); echo "  control run_matches on a command printing one match: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle false);             echo "  control run_matches on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
    
      chmod 644 "$d/locked"
      rm -rf "${d:?}"
      counting_controls_ok=$ok
      echo "  counting controls: $([ "$ok" = yes ] && echo "all as expected" || echo FAILED)"
    }

